---
import TextbookCallout from '../TextbookCallout.astro';
import TextbookChunk from '../TextbookChunk.astro';
import TextbookPageIntro from '../TextbookPageIntro.astro';
import TextbookSectionBlock from '../TextbookSectionBlock.astro';
import type { TextbookChapterModel, TextbookSectionModel } from '../../../lib/textbook-model';

interface Props {
  chapter: TextbookChapterModel;
}

const { chapter } = Astro.props as Props;

const requireSection = (code: string): TextbookSectionModel => {
  const section = chapter.sections.find((candidate) => candidate.code === code);
  if (!section) {
    throw new Error(`Missing textbook section "${code}" for rebuilt chapter ${chapter.code}.`);
  }
  return section;
};

const a21 = requireSection('A2.1');
const a22 = requireSection('A2.2');
const a23 = requireSection('A2.3');
const a24 = requireSection('A2.4');
---

<TextbookPageIntro code={chapter.code} title={chapter.title} summary={chapter.summary} visibility={chapter.visibility}>
  <p>
    A2 connects the visible parts of a network, such as routers, switches, and access points, to the
    less visible rules that make transmission reliable and secure. The chapter moves from network
    purpose and architecture into addressing, packet movement, routing, and the controls used to
    reduce risk.
  </p>
  <TextbookCallout tone="note" label="Reading pattern">
    <p>
      Start by asking what kind of network is being described and what the infrastructure is trying to
      achieve. Then trace how devices, protocols, and addressing move data. Finish by judging how
      far segmentation, firewalls, encryption, and other controls actually improve security.
    </p>
  </TextbookCallout>
</TextbookPageIntro>

<TextbookSectionBlock section={a21}>
  <TextbookChunk
    label="A2.1.1, A2.1.2"
    summary="Describe network types and modern digital infrastructures by linking each one to its scale, purpose, benefits, and limitations."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      A network is a set of connected devices that exchange data using agreed rules. The first step is
      to identify the scale and purpose of the connection. After that, the focus shifts to the larger
      infrastructures that allow services to operate across many locations and devices.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-network-types">Overview: network types</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Network type</th>
          <th scope="col">Typical scope</th>
          <th scope="col">Why it is used</th>
          <th scope="col">Main limitation or consideration</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>LAN</td>
          <td>One building or campus</td>
          <td>High-speed local resource sharing</td>
          <td>Limited geographic reach</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>WAN</td>
          <td>Large geographic area</td>
          <td>Connects multiple LANs over distance</td>
          <td>Higher cost and more dependency on external links</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>PAN</td>
          <td>One person and nearby devices</td>
          <td>Short-range personal connectivity</td>
          <td>Very limited range and capacity</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>VPN</td>
          <td>Private overlay across public infrastructure</td>
          <td>Secure remote access and protected communication</td>
          <td>Performance depends on the underlying internet connection</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="overview-modern-digital-infrastructure">Overview: modern digital infrastructure</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Infrastructure</th>
          <th scope="col">Core idea</th>
          <th scope="col">Benefit</th>
          <th scope="col">Limitation</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>The internet</td>
          <td>A global interconnection of networks</td>
          <td>Worldwide communication and service access</td>
          <td>Depends on shared public infrastructure</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Cloud computing</td>
          <td>Remote provision of storage, processing, and services</td>
          <td>Scalable resources without local ownership of all hardware</td>
          <td>Ongoing cost and provider dependence</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Distributed systems</td>
          <td>Multiple computers cooperate as one wider system</td>
          <td>Improved resilience and workload sharing</td>
          <td>Coordination is more complex</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Edge computing</td>
          <td>Processing happens near the source of the data</td>
          <td>Lower latency and less central traffic</td>
          <td>Devices at the edge may be less powerful</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Mobile networks</td>
          <td>Wireless infrastructure supporting moving devices</td>
          <td>Connectivity without fixed cabling</td>
          <td>Coverage and bandwidth can vary by location</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-matching-network-and-infrastructure">Worked example: matching a network to a context</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Scenario</th>
          <th scope="col">Best fit</th>
          <th scope="col">Why</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Classroom devices sharing files and printers inside one school building</td>
          <td>LAN</td>
          <td>The main need is fast local communication inside a limited site.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Staff working from home and accessing internal school systems</td>
          <td>VPN over the internet</td>
          <td>The VPN provides protected access across public infrastructure.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Smart traffic lights responding immediately to local sensor data</td>
          <td>Edge computing</td>
          <td>Processing near the junction reduces delay and dependency on a distant server.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>A blockchain service replicated across many machines</td>
          <td>Distributed system</td>
          <td>The system depends on multiple nodes sharing and checking the same data.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="A2.1.3"
    summary="Describe the function of network devices by linking each device to its role in moving or controlling traffic."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      Network devices do not all do the same job. Some connect a device to the network, some direct
      packets, and some enforce control at the boundary between trusted and untrusted traffic.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-network-devices">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Device</th>
          <th scope="col">Main function</th>
          <th scope="col">Typical use</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Network interface card (NIC)</td>
          <td>Connects a device to the network medium</td>
          <td>Built into a laptop, desktop, or server</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Wireless access point</td>
          <td>Provides wireless entry to a wired network</td>
          <td>School or office Wi-Fi</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Switch</td>
          <td>Forwards frames inside a local network</td>
          <td>Connects devices inside a LAN</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Router</td>
          <td>Forwards packets between networks</td>
          <td>Connects a school LAN to the wider internet</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Gateway</td>
          <td>Acts as the access point from one network environment to another</td>
          <td>Default path out of a local network</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Modem</td>
          <td>Converts signals for the provider connection</td>
          <td>Links the local network to the ISP service</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Hardware firewall</td>
          <td>Filters traffic according to security rules</td>
          <td>Protects the network boundary</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-trace-device-path">Worked trace: one device path through the network</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Step</th>
          <th scope="col">Device involved</th>
          <th scope="col">Why it matters</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>1</td>
          <td>NIC</td>
          <td>The laptop gains a local network connection.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>2</td>
          <td>Wireless access point</td>
          <td>The wireless signal joins the wired school network.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>3</td>
          <td>Switch</td>
          <td>Traffic is forwarded inside the local network.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>4</td>
          <td>Router or gateway</td>
          <td>The request is directed toward another network.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>5</td>
          <td>Hardware firewall and modem</td>
          <td>Traffic is filtered, then converted for the provider connection.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="A2.1.4"
    summary="Describe the transport and application protocols in scope by explaining what each one contributes to communication."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      Protocols are agreed rules. In this topic, the important distinction is between transport
      protocols, which control end-to-end delivery, and application protocols, which define the service
      or message being exchanged.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-transport-and-application-protocols">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Protocol</th>
          <th scope="col">Type</th>
          <th scope="col">What it does</th>
          <th scope="col">Typical use</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>TCP</td>
          <td>Transport</td>
          <td>Provides ordered, reliable delivery with retransmission if needed</td>
          <td>Web pages, file transfer, login systems</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>UDP</td>
          <td>Transport</td>
          <td>Sends data with low overhead and no guaranteed delivery</td>
          <td>Live streaming, gaming, voice traffic</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>HTTP</td>
          <td>Application</td>
          <td>Transfers standard web requests and responses</td>
          <td>Ordinary web browsing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>HTTPS</td>
          <td>Application</td>
          <td>Transfers web data through an encrypted secure connection</td>
          <td>Banking, school portals, online shopping</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>DHCP</td>
          <td>Application</td>
          <td>Assigns IP settings automatically to joining devices</td>
          <td>Home, school, and office networks</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <TextbookCallout tone="misconception" label="Common misconception">
      <p>
        TCP and HTTP are not alternatives. TCP handles reliable transport, while HTTP or HTTPS sits
        above that transport and defines the web request itself.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>

    <h3 id="worked-example-loading-a-secure-page">Worked example: loading a secure page</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Stage</th>
          <th scope="col">Protocol in focus</th>
          <th scope="col">Purpose</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Device joins the school network</td>
          <td>DHCP</td>
          <td>The device receives an IP address and other configuration settings.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Connection begins</td>
          <td>TCP</td>
          <td>Reliable transport is established for the exchange.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Web request is sent</td>
          <td>HTTPS</td>
          <td>The browser requests secure content from the portal.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Response arrives</td>
          <td>TCP plus HTTPS</td>
          <td>The data is delivered reliably and read securely.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="A2.1.5"
    summary="Describe the TCP/IP model by identifying the role of each layer and how the layers cooperate during transmission."
    visibility="hl"
  >
    <p>
      The TCP/IP model explains network communication by grouping responsibilities into layers. Each
      layer has a different job, and together the layers allow data to move from an application on one
      device to an application on another.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-tcp-ip-model">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Layer</th>
          <th scope="col">Main responsibility</th>
          <th scope="col">Representative example</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Application</td>
          <td>Defines services and message formats for users and software</td>
          <td>HTTP, HTTPS, DHCP</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Transport</td>
          <td>Controls end-to-end delivery behavior</td>
          <td>TCP, UDP</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Internet</td>
          <td>Handles logical addressing and packet forwarding between networks</td>
          <td>IP addressing and routing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Network interface</td>
          <td>Moves frames across the local medium</td>
          <td>Ethernet or Wi-Fi transmission</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-trace-layer-encapsulation">Worked trace: one message through the layers</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Layer</th>
          <th scope="col">What is added or decided</th>
          <th scope="col">Result</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Application</td>
          <td>The browser creates an HTTPS request</td>
          <td>A message ready to be delivered</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Transport</td>
          <td>TCP organizes the data for reliable transmission</td>
          <td>Segments can be checked and re-ordered if needed</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Internet</td>
          <td>IP addresses are attached for routing</td>
          <td>Packets can travel across networks</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Network interface</td>
          <td>The packet is framed for the local medium</td>
          <td>The data is sent over cable or wireless signal</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TextbookChunk>
</TextbookSectionBlock>

<TextbookSectionBlock section={a22}>
  <TextbookChunk
    label="A2.2.1"
    summary="Describe network topologies by linking structure to reliability, speed, scalability, collisions, and cost."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      A topology is the arrangement of connections in a network. The shape matters because it affects
      fault tolerance, traffic flow, expansion, and cost.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-network-topologies">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Topology</th>
          <th scope="col">Main structure</th>
          <th scope="col">Strength</th>
          <th scope="col">Limitation</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Star</td>
          <td>Devices connect through one central device</td>
          <td>Easy to manage and expand</td>
          <td>The central device is a major point of dependence</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Mesh</td>
          <td>Nodes have multiple interconnections</td>
          <td>High reliability if one link fails</td>
          <td>More expensive and complex to maintain</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Hybrid</td>
          <td>Combines different topology patterns</td>
          <td>Can fit varied local needs</td>
          <td>Planning and troubleshooting are more demanding</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-choosing-a-topology">Worked example: choosing a topology</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Scenario</th>
          <th scope="col">Best fit</th>
          <th scope="col">Why</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>School computer room with one managed switch</td>
          <td>Star</td>
          <td>It is cost-effective, scalable, and simple to troubleshoot.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Emergency communication network where uptime matters most</td>
          <td>Mesh</td>
          <td>Alternative paths keep the network working if one link fails.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Large campus with offices, labs, and wireless guest access</td>
          <td>Hybrid</td>
          <td>Different areas can use different structures while staying connected.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="A2.2.2"
    summary="Describe server functions by connecting each server type to the service it provides for the network."
    visibility="hl"
  >
    <p>
      A server is a computer or process that provides a specific network service. In HL, the key point
      is not only what each server does, but why that service improves scalability, reliability, or
      security.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-server-functions">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Server type</th>
          <th scope="col">Function</th>
          <th scope="col">Why it matters</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>DNS server</td>
          <td>Translates domain names into IP addresses</td>
          <td>Users can request services by name instead of by number</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>DHCP server</td>
          <td>Assigns IP configuration automatically</td>
          <td>Reduces manual setup and addressing errors</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>File server</td>
          <td>Stores and shares files centrally</td>
          <td>Supports controlled access and backup</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Mail server</td>
          <td>Manages email sending, receiving, and storage</td>
          <td>Organizes communication as a dedicated service</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Proxy server</td>
          <td>Handles requests on behalf of clients</td>
          <td>Can improve filtering, caching, and security control</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Web server</td>
          <td>Hosts web pages and web applications</td>
          <td>Delivers content to browsers through HTTP or HTTPS</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-server-chain">Worked example: one client request through several servers</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Step</th>
          <th scope="col">Server</th>
          <th scope="col">Outcome</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>1</td>
          <td>DHCP server</td>
          <td>The device receives an IP address and gateway settings.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>2</td>
          <td>DNS server</td>
          <td>The school portal name is resolved into an address.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>3</td>
          <td>Proxy server</td>
          <td>The request is checked against school policy.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>4</td>
          <td>Web server</td>
          <td>The portal page is delivered to the client.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="A2.2.3"
    summary="Compare and contrast client-server and peer-to-peer models by weighing control, reliability, cost, and typical applications."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      Networking models describe where services and resources are located. The contrast is between a
      centralized service structure and a more distributed sharing pattern.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-networking-models">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Model</th>
          <th scope="col">Main idea</th>
          <th scope="col">Strength</th>
          <th scope="col">Drawback</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Client-server</td>
          <td>Clients request resources from dedicated servers</td>
          <td>Strong control, centralized management, clearer security policy</td>
          <td>Server failure or overload can affect many clients</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Peer-to-peer</td>
          <td>Devices share resources directly with one another</td>
          <td>Simple setup and no need for a major central server</td>
          <td>Harder to manage consistently at scale</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-matching-networking-models">Worked example: matching the model to the service</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Application</th>
          <th scope="col">Better fit</th>
          <th scope="col">Reason</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Online banking</td>
          <td>Client-server</td>
          <td>Strong centralized control and security are essential.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Small home file-sharing setup</td>
          <td>Peer-to-peer</td>
          <td>Direct sharing can be enough when the scale is small.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>School email platform</td>
          <td>Client-server</td>
          <td>Managed accounts and reliable service delivery matter more than decentralization.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Blockchain network</td>
          <td>Peer-to-peer</td>
          <td>Many nodes cooperate without one single central service point.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="A2.2.4"
    summary="Explain network segmentation by linking segmentation, subnetting, and VLANs to performance, congestion control, and security."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      Segmentation divides a larger network into smaller parts. That division can improve performance,
      reduce unnecessary traffic, and make access rules easier to manage.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-network-segmentation">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Concept</th>
          <th scope="col">Main idea</th>
          <th scope="col">Why it is useful</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Segmentation</td>
          <td>Divide one network into smaller logical or physical sections</td>
          <td>Reduces congestion and limits how widely traffic spreads</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Subnetting</td>
          <td>Split IP address ranges into smaller subnetworks</td>
          <td>Makes addressing and routing more organized</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>VLANs</td>
          <td>Create separate logical LANs on shared switching hardware</td>
          <td>Separates groups without needing completely separate hardware</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <TextbookCallout tone="misconception" label="Common misconception">
      <p>
        Segmentation is not only about blocking access. It is also a performance tool because it
        reduces unnecessary traffic and helps keep broadcast domains smaller.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>

    <h3 id="worked-example-school-network-segmentation">Worked example: school network segmentation</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Segment</th>
          <th scope="col">Reason for separation</th>
          <th scope="col">Benefit</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Administration VLAN</td>
          <td>Holds sensitive staff and finance systems</td>
          <td>Access can be restricted tightly</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Student classroom VLAN</td>
          <td>Handles routine learning traffic</td>
          <td>Traffic from one class does not flood every other area</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Guest Wi-Fi segment</td>
          <td>Supports visitors without exposing internal services</td>
          <td>Improves security and resource control</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TextbookChunk>
</TextbookSectionBlock>

<TextbookSectionBlock section={a23}>
  <TextbookChunk
    label="A2.3.1"
    summary="Describe IP addressing by distinguishing IPv4 from IPv6 and contrasting public, private, static, and dynamic addresses."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      IP addressing identifies devices and networks. A strong description distinguishes address format,
      visibility, and assignment method rather than treating every IP address as the same kind of label.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-ip-addressing-types">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Addressing idea</th>
          <th scope="col">Meaning</th>
          <th scope="col">Typical implication</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>IPv4</td>
          <td>32-bit addressing system</td>
          <td>Still widely used, but the address space is limited</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>IPv6</td>
          <td>128-bit addressing system</td>
          <td>Provides a much larger address space</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Public IP address</td>
          <td>Routable on the public internet</td>
          <td>Used for communication with external networks</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Private IP address</td>
          <td>Used inside a local network</td>
          <td>Not directly routable on the public internet</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Static IP address</td>
          <td>Remains fixed</td>
          <td>Useful for devices that need a predictable address</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Dynamic IP address</td>
          <td>Assigned automatically</td>
          <td>Simplifies onboarding and routine management</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>NAT</td>
          <td>Translates private internal addresses to public-facing ones</td>
          <td>Reduces public-address demand and hides internal structure</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-nat-in-action">Worked example: NAT in action</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Device or stage</th>
          <th scope="col">Address in use</th>
          <th scope="col">What happens</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Student laptop inside the school</td>
          <td><code>192.168.10.24</code></td>
          <td>The device uses a private address on the local network.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Router to the internet</td>
          <td><code>81.22.14.90</code></td>
          <td>NAT replaces the private source address with the public one.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Reply traffic returns</td>
          <td><code>81.22.14.90</code> then mapped back</td>
          <td>The router restores the internal destination so the reply reaches the laptop.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="A2.3.2"
    summary="Compare fibre optic, twisted pair, and wireless transmission media by weighing bandwidth, cost, range, reliability, and security."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      Transmission media determine how signals travel. Comparison questions are strongest when they
      link the properties of the medium to the context rather than naming one medium as always best.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-data-transmission-media">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Medium</th>
          <th scope="col">Strength</th>
          <th scope="col">Limitation</th>
          <th scope="col">Typical use</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Fibre optic cable</td>
          <td>Very high bandwidth, low attenuation, strong resistance to interference</td>
          <td>Higher installation complexity and cost</td>
          <td>Backbone links and high-capacity connections</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Twisted pair cable</td>
          <td>Affordable and widely used</td>
          <td>Lower bandwidth and shorter effective range than fibre</td>
          <td>Classrooms, offices, and local wired runs</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Wireless transmission</td>
          <td>Flexible and mobile</td>
          <td>More vulnerable to interference, range limits, and security concerns</td>
          <td>Laptops, tablets, phones, and guest access</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-choosing-a-transmission-medium">Worked example: choosing a transmission medium</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Scenario</th>
          <th scope="col">Best fit</th>
          <th scope="col">Reason</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Connect two campus buildings with heavy traffic between them</td>
          <td>Fibre optic</td>
          <td>Bandwidth and distance matter more than installation simplicity.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Wire a standard desktop room inside one building</td>
          <td>Twisted pair</td>
          <td>It provides reliable local connectivity at manageable cost.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Give visitors temporary access in a library or hall</td>
          <td>Wireless</td>
          <td>Mobility and convenience are more important than maximum bandwidth.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="A2.3.3"
    summary="Explain packet switching by showing how data is divided, addressed, forwarded, and reassembled."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      Packet switching sends data as smaller units rather than as one uninterrupted block. Each packet
      can carry control information that helps the network move it toward the destination and rebuild
      the original message at the end.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-packet-switching">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Stage</th>
          <th scope="col">What happens</th>
          <th scope="col">Why it matters</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Segmentation</td>
          <td>Data is split into packets</td>
          <td>Large messages become easier to manage across the network</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Header attachment</td>
          <td>Each packet gains control information such as addressing</td>
          <td>Routers can decide where to send it</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Forwarding</td>
          <td>Switches and routers move packets across the network</td>
          <td>Traffic can share links efficiently</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Reassembly</td>
          <td>Packets are put back into the correct order at the destination</td>
          <td>The original message becomes usable again</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-trace-packet-switching">Worked trace: one file sent as packets</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Packet</th>
          <th scope="col">Route taken</th>
          <th scope="col">Key idea</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>1</td>
          <td>School switch -> router A -> router C</td>
          <td>Packets can be forwarded independently.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>2</td>
          <td>School switch -> router B -> router C</td>
          <td>A different available path may still reach the same destination.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>3</td>
          <td>School switch -> router A -> router C</td>
          <td>The destination uses sequence information to reassemble the data correctly.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="A2.3.4"
    summary="Explain static and dynamic routing by comparing how each method handles configuration, maintenance, and change."
    visibility="hl"
  >
    <p>
      Routing decides the path that packets take. At HL, the comparison matters: static routing is
      manually defined, while dynamic routing adjusts as routers exchange information about the
      network.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-static-vs-dynamic-routing">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Factor</th>
          <th scope="col">Static routing</th>
          <th scope="col">Dynamic routing</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Configuration</td>
          <td>Manual</td>
          <td>Built from router exchanges and route updates</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Maintenance</td>
          <td>Administrator must edit routes when the network changes</td>
          <td>Routers update paths automatically</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Complexity</td>
          <td>Lower in small stable networks</td>
          <td>Higher because routers need more decision-making</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Resource usage</td>
          <td>Low</td>
          <td>Higher CPU, memory, and bandwidth use</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Convergence and fault response</td>
          <td>No automatic recovery</td>
          <td>Routes can be recalculated after a change</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Scalability</td>
          <td>Best for smaller, stable networks</td>
          <td>Better for larger or changing networks</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-routing-failure">Worked example: what happens after a link failure</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Routing mode</th>
          <th scope="col">Behavior after link failure</th>
          <th scope="col">Result</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Static</td>
          <td>Traffic continues to use the stored route until an administrator changes it</td>
          <td>Outage can last longer</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Dynamic</td>
          <td>Routers exchange updates and select a new path after convergence</td>
          <td>Recovery is faster in a changing network</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TextbookChunk>
</TextbookSectionBlock>

<TextbookSectionBlock section={a24}>
  <TextbookChunk
    label="A2.4.1"
    summary="Discuss firewall effectiveness by balancing what firewalls do well against what they cannot solve on their own."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      A firewall inspects traffic and applies rules to decide what should be allowed or blocked. The
      command term is discuss, so the answer must be balanced: firewalls are useful, but they are not a
      complete security solution.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-firewall-effectiveness">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Firewall contribution</th>
          <th scope="col">Why it helps</th>
          <th scope="col">Limit or caution</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Filters inbound and outbound traffic</td>
          <td>Blocks traffic that breaks the defined security policy</td>
          <td>Protection depends on the quality of the rule set</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Uses whitelists, blacklists, and rules</td>
          <td>Allows only approved traffic patterns</td>
          <td>Misconfiguration can create major gaps</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Supports NAT at the boundary</td>
          <td>Reduces exposure of internal private addresses</td>
          <td>NAT improves obscurity, but it is not a full defence by itself</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <TextbookCallout tone="warning" label="Discussion rule">
      <p>
        A good discussion names both strengths and limitations. Firewalls help with traffic control,
        but they do not solve social engineering, weak passwords, or every threat hidden inside allowed
        traffic.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>

    <h3 id="worked-example-school-firewall-policy">Worked example: a school firewall policy</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Rule</th>
          <th scope="col">Purpose</th>
          <th scope="col">What still remains necessary</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Allow outbound HTTPS to approved learning platforms</td>
          <td>Supports secure web access for lessons</td>
          <td>Account security and staff awareness still matter</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Block unsolicited inbound traffic from the public internet</td>
          <td>Reduces direct exposure of student devices</td>
          <td>Internal patching and endpoint protection are still required</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Restrict staff server access to the staff VLAN</td>
          <td>Limits who can reach sensitive systems</td>
          <td>Authentication and monitoring are still needed</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="A2.4.2, A2.4.3"
    summary="Describe common network vulnerabilities and countermeasures by linking each threat to an appropriate defensive response."
    visibility="hl"
  >
    <p>
      Vulnerabilities describe how a network can be attacked or exploited. Countermeasures reduce that
      risk, but no single control solves every threat. The important skill is matching the weakness to
      the most relevant response.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-vulnerabilities-and-countermeasures">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Vulnerability</th>
          <th scope="col">Typical effect</th>
          <th scope="col">Representative countermeasure</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>DDoS</td>
          <td>Overwhelms a service with traffic</td>
          <td>DDoS mitigation tools and traffic filtering</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Insecure protocols</td>
          <td>Traffic can be exposed or altered more easily</td>
          <td>Encrypted protocols such as HTTPS or TLS-based services</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Malware</td>
          <td>Damages systems, steals data, or spreads further</td>
          <td>Email filtering, patching, and careful update practice</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>MitM attack</td>
          <td>Communication is intercepted or changed</td>
          <td>TLS or SSL certificates and secure authentication</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Phishing</td>
          <td>Users are tricked into revealing credentials</td>
          <td>Email filtering, MFA, and staff training</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>SQL injection</td>
          <td>Database actions become possible through unsafe input</td>
          <td>Input validation and whitelisting</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>XSS</td>
          <td>Malicious scripts run in trusted pages</td>
          <td>Input validation and content security policies</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Unpatched software</td>
          <td>Known weaknesses remain open</td>
          <td>Regular updates and security testing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Weak authentication</td>
          <td>Accounts are easier to compromise</td>
          <td>Complex password policies and MFA</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Zero-day exploit</td>
          <td>Attack occurs before a patch is available</td>
          <td>Layered controls, IDS, IPS, and rapid response planning</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <TextbookCallout tone="note" label="Important supporting controls">
      <p>
        The guide also expects regular security testing and employee training. For wireless security,
        MAC-based allow lists or block lists may be used as additional access controls.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>

    <h3 id="worked-example-matching-threats-and-controls">Worked example: matching threats and controls</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Threat in one school platform</th>
          <th scope="col">Most relevant control</th>
          <th scope="col">Why it fits</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Bot traffic floods the parent portal</td>
          <td>DDoS mitigation tool</td>
          <td>The main problem is volume and service availability.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>A fake email tries to steal staff passwords</td>
          <td>Email filtering plus MFA</td>
          <td>Filtering reduces exposure, and MFA limits damage if a password is stolen.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>A form accepts unsafe text and alters database queries</td>
          <td>Input validation and whitelisting</td>
          <td>The defence must stop unsafe input before it reaches the query.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>An outdated server still runs a known weakness</td>
          <td>Update software and test regularly</td>
          <td>The exposure remains until patching and review are done.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="A2.4.4"
    summary="Describe encryption and digital certificates by distinguishing symmetric and asymmetric cryptography and explaining how certificates support secure connections."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      Encryption protects data by making it unreadable without the correct key. Digital certificates
      help confirm that a public key belongs to the organization or server it claims to represent.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-encryption-and-certificates">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Idea</th>
          <th scope="col">How it works</th>
          <th scope="col">Why it is used</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Symmetric cryptography</td>
          <td>The same key encrypts and decrypts the data</td>
          <td>Efficient for protecting data once both sides share the key</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Asymmetric cryptography</td>
          <td>A public key encrypts and a private key decrypts, or vice versa for verification</td>
          <td>Supports secure key exchange and identity checking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Digital certificate</td>
          <td>Binds a public key to a verified identity</td>
          <td>Helps a browser trust that it is connecting to the correct server</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <TextbookCallout tone="misconception" label="Common misconception">
      <p>
        Encryption alone does not prove who is on the other end of the connection. The certificate is
        what helps establish trust in the server identity.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>

    <h3 id="worked-example-https-certificate-process">Worked example: a secure website connection</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Step</th>
          <th scope="col">What happens</th>
          <th scope="col">Why it matters</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>1</td>
          <td>The browser requests a secure connection</td>
          <td>The session begins under HTTPS rather than plain HTTP.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>2</td>
          <td>The server sends its digital certificate</td>
          <td>The browser can check the claimed identity and public key.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>3</td>
          <td>The browser verifies the certificate and uses the public key</td>
          <td>A secure exchange can be established without sending the private key.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>4</td>
          <td>Data is exchanged in encrypted form</td>
          <td>Confidential information is protected in transit.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TextbookChunk>
</TextbookSectionBlock>
